        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-fig01",
          "kind": "figure",
          "reveal": "fade"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth",
          "definition": "A pointer to evidence; distinct from truth, a property of the world.",
          "term": "grounding"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-p05",
          "definition": "Does E entail c? A judgment call every downstream verdict depends on.",
          "term": "entailment"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-p04",
          "definition": "In {supported, contradicted, insufficient}; assumes trustworthy evidence.",
          "term": "support(c, E)"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 4,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-p03",
          "answer": "A pointer can aim at a wrong page (grounded but false); a true statement can lack a pointer (true but ungrounded). The course cares about the pointer because checks need it.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-r1",
          "question": "How do grounding and truth differ?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-p12",
          "answer": "Break the trustworthy-evidence assumption: a forum post with the wrong refund window makes the answer grounded and false. A quote taken out of context gives a pointer without support.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c01-grounding-versus-truth-r2",
          "question": "When is grounded not true?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C01: grounding versus truth"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-fig01",
          "kind": "figure",
          "reveal": "fade"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-p01",
          "definition": "The angle between meaning coordinates, ignoring length.",
          "term": "cosine similarity"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-p01",
          "definition": "Dot product equals cosine only on unit vectors; normalize first.",
          "term": "normalization"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-p04",
          "definition": "In R^d; task-relevant similarity must match geometric closeness.",
          "term": "e(x)"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 4,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-p02",
          "answer": "Query [1.0, 0.0]; d1 cos 0.90, d2 0.70, d3 0.00, d4 -0.60. Rank: d1, d2, d3, d4.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-r1",
          "question": "How does the toy rank the four docs?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-p12",
          "answer": "Break the training assumption: a news-trained embedder puts legal consideration near thoughtfulness instead of payment. Geometry reflects the training corpus, not your task.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c02-embeddings-r2",
          "question": "What breaks embeddings?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C02: embeddings"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-p03",
          "definition": "Fraction of true top-k found; the price of approximate indexing.",
          "term": "recall"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-p03",
          "definition": "p partitions, probe w; cost near O(w n d / p).",
          "term": "partitions"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-p02",
          "definition": "Exact search on 10M docs costs about 15 GFLOPs per query.",
          "term": "GFLOPs"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 4,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-p02",
          "answer": "Exact search: 10M x 768 = 7.7e9 multiply-adds per query, about 15 GFLOPs; 150 GFLOPs/s at 10 queries per second. Approximate indexing trades a little recall for a lot of speed.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-r1",
          "question": "What does one query cost at 10M docs?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-p12",
          "answer": "Break the partition assumption: a query at a cluster boundary misses its nearest neighbor with w = 1. Stale embeddings after a model update break the index.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c03-vector-stores-r2",
          "question": "What breaks vector stores?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C03: vector stores"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-p01",
          "definition": "Insurance at the cuts; any span shorter than o survives uncut.",
          "term": "overlap"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-p01",
          "definition": "With overlap o, a span is cut only if a boundary falls inside it.",
          "term": "boundary"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-p04",
          "definition": "Chunk size s tokens, overlap o tokens, count near n / (s - o).",
          "term": "s tokens"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 3,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-p02",
          "answer": "Fixed 200-token chunks on a 1000-token doc give 5 chunks. The answer span at tokens 190-210 splits across chunks 1 and 2; neither chunk alone supports the answer.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-r1",
          "question": "What goes wrong with fixed chunks?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-p12",
          "answer": "Break the one-chunk assumption: an answer needing facts from pages 3 and 7 cannot win with single-chunk retrieval. Multi-hop answers need multi-chunk assembly.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c04-chunks-r2",
          "question": "What breaks chunking?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C04: chunks"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-fig01",
          "kind": "figure",
          "reveal": "fade"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense",
          "definition": "Two witnesses: lexical catches exact words, dense catches meaning.",
          "term": "hybrid"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-p01",
          "definition": "12.5 on the refund policy doc; the lexical signal.",
          "term": "BM25"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-p04",
          "definition": "Divide each score by its list max, then fuse: s = w s_lex + (1 - w) s_dense.",
          "term": "normalization"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 4,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-p05",
          "answer": "Raw BM25 and cosine live on different scales, so w is uninterpretable without normalization. After normalization, w = 0.5 is an honest even vote between witnesses.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-r1",
          "question": "Why normalize before fusion?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-p12",
          "answer": "Break the normalization assumption: raw BM25 (0-30) at w = 0.5 silences the dense witness. Pure-stopword queries make lexical scores noise.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c05-hybrid-lexicaldense-r2",
          "question": "What breaks hybrid search?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C05: hybrid lexical/dense"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-fig01",
          "kind": "figure",
          "reveal": "fade"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-p02",
          "definition": "Bob's ACL: {d2, d3}; unfiltered top-1 d1 is a leak.",
          "term": "ACL"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-p05",
          "definition": "Apply the predicate before scoring; post-filtering still ships text through the pipeline.",
          "term": "pre-filtering"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions",
          "definition": "The card catalog: date, author, section, access list.",
          "term": "metadata"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 4,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-p03",
          "answer": "Filter first, then rank. The model must never see text the user may not see, because the model cannot unsee it. Pre-filtering keeps forbidden text out of the pipeline entirely.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-r1",
          "question": "Why filter before scoring?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-p12",
          "answer": "Break the current-ACL assumption: Bob left HR yesterday but the index still lists him. Metadata labeled public quoting a private email leaks through the label.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c06-metadatapermissions-r2",
          "question": "What breaks permissions?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C06: metadata/permissions"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder",
          "definition": "One careful reader with both pages open; slower, sees the interaction.",
          "term": "cross-encoder"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-p02",
          "definition": "Two speed-readers who never meet; they compare notes as vectors.",
          "term": "bi-encoder"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-p04",
          "definition": "Per-pair cost; the budget assumption that limits k.",
          "term": "O((n_q + n_d)^2 d)"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 3,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-p02",
          "answer": "Bi-encoder ranks d2 (0.91) over d1 (0.82). Cross-encoder reads both together: d1 0.95, d2 0.30, d5 0.60. Reranked top-1: d1.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-r1",
          "question": "How does the cross-encoder flip the ranking?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-p12",
          "answer": "Break the budget: k = 1000 makes the reranker cost more than the pipeline. Short-passage training truncates on 2000-token docs.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c07-cross-encoder-r2",
          "question": "What breaks cross-encoders?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C07: cross-encoder"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction",
          "definition": "Keeps every token vector; decides at query time.",
          "term": "late interaction"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-p01",
          "definition": "Each query term finds its evidence independently.",
          "term": "max-sim"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-p04",
          "definition": "E_q in R^(m x d), E_d in R^(n x d); score = sum_i max_j S_ij.",
          "term": "S = E_q E_d^T"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 3,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-p05",
          "answer": "The max lets each query term find its evidence independently: refund matches refund exactly (1.0) even with within days between them. Nothing is averaged away before the match.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-r1",
          "question": "Why does late interaction beat one vector?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-p12",
          "answer": "Break the counting assumption: refund repeated four times maxes every query token at 1.0 and ties the good doc. Max-sim cannot count distinct evidence.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c08-colbert-late-interaction-r2",
          "question": "What breaks late interaction?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C08: ColBERT late interaction"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-p05",
          "definition": "Cheap x k1 + expensive x k2; the cascade operating point.",
          "term": "cascade"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-p04",
          "definition": "First stage returns k1 candidates; the gold must be inside.",
          "term": "k1"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-p03",
          "definition": "The first stage must not lose the answer; the second picks the winner.",
          "term": "recall"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 3,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-p03",
          "answer": "First stage fast and rough (recall), second stage slow and careful (precision). Set k1 large enough for recall, k2 small enough for latency; total cost = cheap x k1 + expensive x k2.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-r1",
          "question": "How do you set the cascade operating point?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-p12",
          "answer": "Break the recall assumption: gold at rank 101 with k1 = 100 is lost forever. An untrained-domain reranker makes the pipeline pay 10x for noise.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c09-rerank-r2",
          "question": "What breaks reranking?"
        }
      ],
      "title": "cs329z-U02-C09: rerank"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-p03",
          "kind": "keyIdea",
          "reveal": "rise"
        }
      ],
      "endAnchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-end",
      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-p01",
          "definition": "A receipt: this claim came from this span; checkable without rerunning.",
          "term": "citation"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-p02",
          "definition": "Document-level is too coarse; span-level is falsifiable in seconds.",
          "term": "span"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-p03",
          "definition": "(doc id, chunk id, span); quality = fraction of claims with a correct pointer.",
          "term": "receipt"
        }
      ],
      "minutes": 3,
      "recall": [
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-p02",
          "answer": "[1] must point to chunk 2, tokens 150-350, containing the refund sentence. Claims without receipts are assertions; the user decides trust.",
          "id": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-r1",
          "question": "What must a citation contain?"
        },
        {
          "anchor": "cs329z-u02-c10-citations-p12",
          "answer": "Break the span assumption: the supporting sentence sits in chunk 3, not cited chunk 2. Correct citations plus one uncited sentence create false confidence in the whole answer.",
